            "Your amateur radio callsign: it appears in the Live position and +- 24h tab, on the map and in the window title.",
            "Amateur radio callsign",
            "The new language is applied at restart. Do you want to restart YAEC now?",
            "Calculator for amateur radio EME (Earth-Moon-Earth) traffic. Web addresses are clickable.",
            "CREDITS FOR THE ALGORITHMS, FORMULAS AND DATA USED",
            "VERSION HISTORY",
            "NOTES",
            "The listed algorithms were rewritten in C# for this program: it contains no compiled external libraries. The embedded data tables (sky temperature, time zones, Moon image) were converted to a compact form from the original files listed. Values marked as \"indicative\", \"experimental\" or \"BETA\" are meant for comparison and planning, not as measurements.",
            "The program source code was written with the assistance of Claude (Anthropic) following the directions of Costantino IC8TEM.",
            "Moon ephemerides",
            "Jean Meeus, \"Astronomical Algorithms\", 2nd ed., Willmann-Bell 1998: ch. 47 (ELP-2000/82 theory by M. Chapront-Touzé and J. Chapront, truncated version), ch. 22 (nutation), ch. 12 (sidereal time), ch. 40 (topocentric correction and parallax), ch. 13 (azimuth and elevation), ch. 7 (Julian day).",
            "Sun position",
            "J. Meeus, \"Astronomical Algorithms\", ch. 25 (low accuracy method, about 0.01°).",
            "Atmospheric refraction",
            "Sæmundsson formula (Sky & Telescope, 1986), as given by Meeus in ch. 16.",
            "TT - UT difference (ΔT)",
            "Polynomial approximations by F. Espenak and J. Meeus (NASA).",
            "Tsky along the ecliptic, Dgrd, spatial polarization offset (Dpol), MNR",
            "WSJT-X by Joe Taylor K1JT and the WSJT Development Group, file lib/astro.f90 (GNU GPL v3 licence): sky temperature table at 144 MHz, degradation formula, polarization angle and maximum non-reciprocity, rewritten in C#.",
            "Doppler and CFOM method",
            "Doppler computed from the topocentric radial velocity of the Moon; CFOM scheme (Constant Frequency On the Moon) as in the WSJT-X Doppler tracking modes.",
            "Radar equation with lunar reflectivity 0.065 and lunar radius 1737.4 km (standard values of the EME literature).",
            "Receiver noise figure (Friis formula)",
            "H.T. Friis, \"Noise Figures of Radio Receivers\", Proc. IRE 32, 1944; formulation and practical use according to Hewlett-Packard / Agilent (now Keysight) Application Note 57-1, \"Fundamentals of RF and Microwave Noise Figure Measurements\" (5952-8255E). The radio noise figure is estimated from its MDS with the bandwidth set by the user: NF = MDS + 174 - 10·log(B).",
            "Ground gain (BETA, antenna without elevation)",
            "Two-ray model over flat, perfectly reflecting ground, horizontal polarization: G = 20·log|2·sin(2π·h·sin(El)/λ)|, first lobe at asin(λ/4h).",
            "Sky temperature over the whole celestial sphere",
            "Global Sky Model: A. de Oliveira-Costa et al., \"A model of diffuse Galactic radio emission from 10 MHz to 100 GHz\", MNRAS 388, 247 (2008), based among others on the 408 MHz survey by C.G.T. Haslam et al. (1982). \"408 MHz locked\" version distributed with the LWA Software Library (LSL), resampled to 2° and computed at 144 MHz.",
            "Moon image (start-up window)",
            "Lunar disc derived from the photographic Moon map \"moon_1024.jpg\" distributed with the three.js library, projected as seen from the Earth.",
            "Radio sources",
            "J2000 positions from standard catalogues (3C and others); indicative fluxes at about 160 MHz scaled with typical spectral indices.",
            "Galactic coordinates",
            "J2000 north galactic pole (RA 192.85948°, Dec 27.12825°, l of the celestial pole 122.93192°), IAU definition.",
            "Solar flux (SFU)",
            "NOAA Space Weather Prediction Center, file solar-radio-flux.json; measurements of the U.S. Air Force RSTN network: San Vito dei Normanni, Learmonth, Sagamore Hill, Kaena Point.",
            "Time zones from position",
            "tz-lookup by DarkSky / PhotoStructure (CC0 1.0 licence) for the time zone map; windowsZones.xml table of the Unicode CLDR project (Unicode licence) for the mapping to Windows time zones.",
            "World maps",
            "© OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL data), OpenTopoMap (CC-BY-SA), CARTO basemaps. Mercator projection and OpenStreetMap tile scheme.",
            "Maidenhead locator and ADIF files",
            "Maidenhead locator system (IARU); ADIF specification (Amateur Data Interchange Format).",
            "Phone app (beta 7, beta 8)",
            "Same lunar ephemeris algorithms rewritten in JavaScript; Progressive Web App with service worker for offline use.",
            "v0 - Moon position from WWL locator, azimuth and elevation limits, table and useful windows, CSV export.",
            "beta 1 - Second station (DX), common windows, Dpol, MNR, Tsky and Dgrd as in WSJT-X.",
            "beta 2 - EME budget tab: EIRP, path loss, received signal and margin over MDS.",
            "beta 3 - Estimated S/N in the budget; Solar noise tab with SFU web data and choice of the RSTN station.",
            "beta 4 - DX local time derived from position; duration in days; column tooltips; Doppler and CFOM frequencies; Sky view tab.",
            "beta 5 - Status columns replaced by colour; title \"by Costantino IC8TEM\"; kHz drop-down for the CFOM frequency.",
            "beta 6 - WWL map and ADIF tab with Maidenhead grid.",
            "beta 7, beta 8 - Phone app (Android and iPhone), beta 8 usable offline and publishable on WordPress.",
            "beta 9 - Sky view in azimuth/elevation with flat horizon and noise colours from blue to red; Distance and Dgrd tab; ABOUT tab.",
            "beta 10 - Tracking tab; kHz from 000 to 999; day of the week; bandwidth and S/N with the Sun in the budget; BETA ground gain; transverter-radio jumper loss; dark theme and fonts; one-month Dgrd with new Moon; LoTW in green; INSTRUCTIONS tab; start-up window and exit confirmation.",
            "beta 11 - Main Tracking screen redesigned (my and DX locator, frequency, date and times, my and DX azimuth and elevation, other useful data); locator and frequency copied automatically to all tabs; receiver noise figure with the Friis formula; Distance and Dgrd up to 61 days; callsign in the general settings; ALC recommendations in the instructions.",
            "beta 12 - The radio noise figure is derived from its MDS with the bandwidth chosen by the user (the same as for the S/N calculation).",
            "beta 13 - Fix of compilation error CS0051 (accessibility of the Friis function).",
            "beta 14 - New name YAEC - Yet Another EME Calc; user interface in 8 languages (English, Italian, German, French, Spanish, Portuguese, Chinese, Japanese) with English as start-up language; callsign in the window title.",
            "YAEC USER INSTRUCTIONS",
            "WHY THIS PROGRAM",
            "YAEC was created to have in a single program all the details useful for operating an EME station, especially when the station has constraints: an antenna fixed in elevation (no elevation rotator), a rotator with limited travel, QTH obstructions (buildings, trees, mountains, the coastline) that hide part of the sky. The most common EME programs tell you where the Moon is; YAEC also tells you whether the Moon can really be reached by YOUR antenna, when it will be, in which conditions (distance, sky noise, polarization, Sun) and how much signal you can expect.",
            "FIRST STEPS",
            "1. In the Live position and +- 24h tab enter your WWL locator, that of the station you want to listen to (DX) and the frequency (MHz + kHz). These data are copied automatically to all tabs, and if you change them in another tab they are updated here too. If the MHz match one of the selected bands, the EME budget switches automatically to that band (and vice versa).",
            "2. In Moon position set the height and the limits of your antenna: azimuth sector (clockwise, from → to) and minimum and maximum elevation. Examples: antenna fixed on the horizon = elevation from 0 to 0 (or from 0 to 10 for the lobe); obstacles up to 8° = minimum elevation 8; rotator that does not exceed 60° = maximum elevation 60.",
            "3. Choose the frequency: MHz and kHz (the CFOM frequency, e.g. 144 and 180 = 144.180 MHz).",
            "4. If you want to plan a QSO, activate the second station and enter the DX locator and limits.",
            "5. Press \"Compute table\": green rows are the useful ones, the box at the bottom lists the windows.",
            "THE TABS",
            "Live position and +- 24h",
            "The main screen, updated every second: your and DX locator, frequency, UTC time, local time and DX local time. Below, the status band (green = pointable, orange = outside the antenna limits, grey = below the horizon, blue = common window with the DX) with the countdown; the azimuth compass and the elevation arc with your antenna limits in yellow and the DX direction; on the right the Moon phase with rise and set, echo and TX/RX CFOM frequencies, Tsky, distance, declination, hour angle, the Dgrd bar, the estimated S/N (with the complex data box) and the DX data; at the bottom the bar of the next 24 hours with the useful and common windows.",
            "Moon position",
            "Table for the chosen period (in days, with the interval in minutes) with the pointability colour; hovering the mouse over a column header shows its explanation. CSV export for Excel.",
            "EME budget",
            "Link budget for each selected band (50 MHz to 122 GHz), with the data of each band stored separately: EIRP, path loss, signal along the receive chain up to the radio (including the jumpers between transverter and radio), margin over MDS, noise and S/N in the chosen bandwidth, compared with the S/N computed with the Sun. With \"BETA- antenna without elevation\" the gain includes the ground gain, computed at the Moon elevation and at the antenna height above ground: the typical case of antennas fixed on the horizon working the Moon at moonrise and moonset. Below the BETA box the overall receiver noise figure computed with the Friis formula is shown (losses, preamp, cable, transverter, jumpers and radio): if it is much higher than that of the preamp alone, the preamp gain is not enough to cover the noise of the following stages. The radio noise figure is derived from its MDS with the bandwidth you choose in the top bar (NF = MDS + 174 - 10·log B): for a correct result choose the bandwidth in which your radio MDS is specified (usually 500 Hz).",
            "Solar noise",
            "Downloads from the web the solar fluxes measured by the RSTN network and estimates how much the Sun increases the receiver noise, based on the angular distance between Sun and Moon and on the antenna pattern. Useful near new Moon.",
            "Sky view",
            "The sky seen from your QTH, with flat horizon and the sky noise colours (from blue to red) at the chosen frequency, your antenna limits, the DX horizon, the radio sources, the Sun and the Moon path over the next 24 hours.",
            "Distance and Dgrd",
            "Chart from 7 to 61 days of Dgrd (0 dB at the bottom, -15 dB at the top) and of the daily mean Moon distance, with perigee, apogee, new Moon and the three best days (★). The ranking considers only the hours in which the Moon is really pointable with your limits.",
            "WWL map and ADIF",
            "World map with the Maidenhead grid; loading the ADIF file of your log colours the worked squares (orange), confirmed via LoTW (green) or by QSL only (blue), also per band and EME only.",
            "ABOUT and general settings",
            "Amateur radio callsign, language, light or dark colour scheme, font size, credits with links and version history.",
            "ADVICE FOR CONSTRAINED STATIONS",
            "• Antenna fixed on the horizon: set the elevation from 0 to the upper limit of the lobe (e.g. 0-12°), activate \"BETA- antenna without elevation\" in the budget with the real height above ground and look for the best days in the Distance and Dgrd tab: windows will be short, at moonrise and moonset.",
            "• QTH obstructions: if an obstacle covers only a sector, narrow the useful azimuth sector or raise the minimum elevation; check the result in the Sky view (yellow lines).",
            "• For a QSO with a DX, the COMMON windows count (green rows with the DX active) and the Dgrd of the more disadvantaged station.",
            "• Near new Moon check the Solar noise tab: with the Sun close to the Moon the noise can rise by many dB.",
            "USE OF ALC IN TRANSMIT IN DIGITAL MODES (JT65, Q65, FT8, MSK144...)",
            "In digital modes the radio transmits a constant-envelope audio signal: it must come out clean, without compression. ALC (automatic level control) is meant to limit voice peaks; if it acts on a digital signal it distorts its envelope, generates intermodulation products and spurious lines on the sides of the signal and, with a linear amplifier after it, amplifies them. In EME, where high power and long transmissions are used, this means a dirty signal, interference to nearby stations and needless stress on the final stages.",
            "• Set the audio level (from the program and the sound card) so that the radio ALC indicator stays at zero or barely moves, at the wanted power.",
            "• Set the power with the power control of the radio (or transverter), not by raising the audio until the ALC acts.",
            "• Use data mode (USB-D / DATA) with a wide and flat TX filter; disable compressor, speech processor and TX equalizer.",
            "• With a transverter, adapt the drive level at the transverter input (attenuator or dedicated adjustment) instead of reducing the audio to the limit: the transverter must not be saturated.",
            "• With a linear amplifier, check that it is not overdriven; if the amplifier has an ALC output to the exciter, use it only as protection, not as power control.",
            "• Check the transmitted signal with a second receiver or an analyser: a single clean line must appear, without side lines.",
            "• Consider the EME transmit duty cycle (up to 50% of the time and long periods): check amplifier cooling and, if necessary, reduce the power.",
            "WARNINGS",
            "Moon positions have an accuracy of about 0.01°. Tsky, Dgrd, ground gain, solar noise and radio source fluxes are model-based estimates: they are meant to compare situations and choose the best moments, they do not replace measurements at the station.",
