            "If you would like to make other transverters or your own transverter known, write an e-mail to the author: the data will be added to chapter 3.7.2 of the manual, citing the source.",
            "The program is under the patronage of the",
            "Licence",
            "YAEC is free software, written by a radio amateur for radio amateurs. Its code was also developed with the help of an artificial intelligence assistant, and the licence follows its way of working: be really useful, always say how things are, show your own reasoning, harm no one and acknowledge the work of others. Below are the principles and then the actual conditions.",
            "Principles",
            "Useful and free",
            "The program is free and you can use it without limits for your amateur radio activity, for study and for teaching. It is meant to make QSOs happen, not to sell something.",
            "Transparent",
            "The source is always delivered with the program; every formula is visible in the DEBUG FORMULE tab and every data item gives its source. No black boxes.",
            "Honest",
            "Measured, derived and estimated values are kept apart; what is not verified is declared as such. An uncertain result is shown as uncertain, not presented as certain.",
            "Respectful",
            "It respects the work of those who measured, wrote and published before us, the privacy of its users (no data collected, no advertising) and the rules of the amateur radio service.",
            "Conditions",
            "Use.",
            "You may use, copy and install YAEC free of charge, on as many computers as you like.",
            "Changes.",
            "You may study the source, fix it and improve it for your own use or for your radio club.",
            "Redistribution.",
            "You may redistribute the program, original or modified, only together with its complete source and under this same licence.",
            "Declaring the changes.",
            "A modified version must say so clearly in the title or in the release notes, stating who modified it and what changed, so that nobody mistakes your work for the author's, or vice versa.",
            "Attribution.",
            "The author's name (Costantino IC8TEM), the “Sources, data and credits” chapter of the manual and all the third-party source citations must be kept: data, tables, articles and measurements belong to those who produced them.",
            "No commercial use.",
            "Selling YAEC or derived versions, or including it in paid products, is not allowed without the written permission of the author.",
            "No hidden data.",
            "No distributed version may collect user data, show advertising or send information to third parties without saying so openly.",
            "Proper use of the radio.",
            "The program helps planning: the frequencies, powers and modes used remain the responsibility of the operator and of his or her amateur radio licence.",
            "No warranty.",
            "YAEC is provided as it is. The calculations are made with care and with declared sources, but they may contain errors: check the important data before relying on them.",
            "Reports.",
            "If you find an error or a better data item, write to the author (AUTHOR CONTACTS AND ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS tab): it will be corrected, citing you as the source.",
            "In short:",
            "use it freely, learn from the code, improve it and share it, but always with the source, with the sources cited, saying what you changed and without selling it. If you doubt a result, the right answer is to check, not to trust.",
            "YAEC licence, version 1",
            "The text is translated into all the program languages; in case of doubt the Italian version prevails.",
            "The main screen, updated every second. At the top the general settings (callsign, language, colours, font sizes and font), the BAND SELECTION button and the complex-data box; below, your and the DX locators (4 to 14 characters, with the accuracy of the position), the frequency and the UTC, local and DX clocks. The status band (green = pointable, orange = outside the limits, grey = below the horizon, blue = common window) has the countdown; then the compass and the elevation arc with the antenna limits in yellow and four cards: Moon (phase, rise and set), Echo and frequencies (Doppler, echo delay and CFOM frequencies), Sky and geometry (Tsky, distance, declination, hour angle, Dgrd and the GO TO SKY VIEW button) and estimated S/N (in 2500 Hz with the typical threshold of the mode as a reference, DX data, the IN-DEPTH CALCULATIONS box and the GO TO CALCULATIONS button). At the bottom the next 24 hours.",
            "Reconnaissance of my obstacles",
            "At the top right two sliders set the opacity and the tone of the grey over the zones outside the limits (the tone also applies to the Sky view).",
            "Link budget for each chosen band (50 to 432 MHz), with the data of each band stored separately. On the left the two stations (antenna and gain, BETA for antennas without elevation), six indicators (QSO possible, EIRP, path loss, signal at the antenna, S/N and S/N in 2500 Hz with the typical threshold of the mode) and the table with all the steps; on the right the diagram of the transmit and receive system of the chosen station: antenna and Tant, antenna – relay cable, coaxial relay, transmitter with its cable, preamplifier, downlead cable, optional transverter, cables and radio with MDS and mode. The cables are chosen from the database of the COAXIAL CABLES tab and the losses are calculated automatically. At the top right the IN-DEPTH CALCULATIONS box, the same as on the main screen.",
            "At the top four indicators, on the left the data source (NOAA RSTN recommended, or F10.7 from NOAA or Penticton, or a custom address) with the stations, on the right the solar flux spectrum with the extrapolation to the working frequency.",
            "This manual, inside the program and in all languages: clickable index, search, formulas and sources.",
            "It is laid out like a Word document, with the formulas in Equation Editor style: it can be copied to the clipboard or saved as Word (.docx).",
            "The tabs are gathered in six groups in the bar at the top: Live (Live position and +- 24h, Reconnaissance of my obstacles, Sky view), Planning (Distance and Dgrd, Moon position), Link (EME budget, Solar noise, Export calculations to Excel, DEBUG FORMULE), Log (WWL map and ADIF), Help and tools (IN-DEPTH USER MANUAL, THEORY AND TIPS ON EME, COAXIAL CABLES), Credits and thanks (ABOUT & CREDITS, AUTHOR CONTACTS AND ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS, LICENCE). Click a group in the first row and then the tab in the second; each group remembers the last tab opened. The grey tabs are enabled with the \"IN-DEPTH CALCULATIONS\" box.",
            "Thank you for using me: I hope I have been useful to you.",
