            elenchi["en"]["C4"] = new string[] {
                "## Moon position",
                "The geocentric position of the Moon is computed with the ELP-2000/82 theory in the abridged form by J. Meeus (Astronomical Algorithms, ch. 47): longitude, latitude and distance from the periodic series, with the dynamical time correction (Delta T). Then come nutation and the obliquity of the ecliptic, the conversion to equatorial coordinates, the apparent sidereal time and the topocentric parallax correction (ch. 40), with the Earth ellipsoid and the station height. The accuracy is about 0.01°.",
                "The Maidenhead locator (4, 6, 8 or 10 characters) is converted to the centre of its square. Azimuth is counted from North towards East. Atmospheric refraction, when enabled, is computed with Sæmundsson's formula R = 1.02 / tan(h + 10.3/(h + 5.11)) arcminutes; otherwise the elevation is geometric, as in the usual EME programs.",
                "## Pointability and windows",
                "Every instant is compared with the antenna limits (clockwise azimuth sector and elevation range) of your station and, when enabled, of the DX: green = pointable, orange = outside the limits, grey = below the horizon. Useful and common windows are the consecutive intervals of pointable instants.",
